cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use exosat-rv in your research, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "exosat-rv: a Python workflow for radial-velocity exosatellite searches around directly imaged companions"
abstract: >-
  A Python toolkit and research workflow for companion-side radial-velocity searches using
  public ESO archive data. It inventories CRIRES+ holdings, converts ESO ADP spectra into the
  cr2res layout consumed by viper, tests alias structure and model comparisons, and provides
  feasibility calculations; accompanying scripts drive the from-raw cr2res/viper analysis. In
  a separately implemented extraction calibrated against the published CD-35 2722 B RV series,
  a downstream search recovers the reported approximately 171-day signal on the 17 nights
  retained by an internal quality screen, while the all-18-night series degrades to noise. Its
  model fits do not ingest the published RV values, but the historical driver constructs a
  paper-matched subset and hard-codes a 171.45-day reporting window; the search is target- and
  paper-aware. The result is therefore conditional on the screen and is not an end-to-end
  independent reproduction. Signal injection shifts an already-built template and validates fitter-stage
  transmission, not possible absorption during template construction. The apparent transfer
  to staring mode was withdrawn after those data were identified as HiRISE fibre observations
  reduced with a slit recipe. The adopted M14/M15 RV, per-order/BERV, parameter, and target
  artifacts are frozen in data/repro with the VIPER configuration and tracked source patch
  observed in the audited checkout, plus a hash manifest. That configuration records checkout
  state only; it does not prove which configuration governed the historical extraction runs.
  Raw/reduced ESO spectra and fitted templates remain external;
  the templates are hash-bound, so Git alone does not replay raw exposures through template
  construction. No discovery is claimed and nothing has been submitted.
